{"id": "37731cf4-2737-40f3-bdbd-51e034244eb5", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"id": "tooluse_v6U3i2AslCtvotVaQLWQig", "tool_name": "gather_context", "input": {"prompt": "{
  "task": "GPU evidence coverage audit (Mode C) for AWS ParallelCluster 'distributed-training-triage-b200' in us-west-2, account 111122223333. Determine whether there were any NVIDIA Xid or GPU errors on the compute nodes over the last 3 days, and prove log coverage before reporting any 'no errors found'.",
  "context": "User wants to rule out GPU/hardware issues before blaming the training application for a problem. This is a coverage-audit style question ('were there any GPU errors, can I trust the logs') so run Steps 1-3 then 6-7 of the aiml-gpu-training-cluster-investigation skill: inventory the cluster and compute nodes, find every candidate CloudWatch log group carrying kernel messages (search by substring for the cluster name, 'kernel', 'messages', 'syslog', 'system', 'dmesg', 'journal', 'gpu' -- not just /aws/parallelcluster prefix), identify the exact log stream per node that carries 'kernel:' lines, then prove that exact stream was live every hour across the padded window (window start minus 1h to window end plus 1h). Search for 'NVRM: Xid' lines in every source found. Also check AWS Health events for EC2 in this account/region, EC2 instance status checks and scheduled events, and CloudTrail for BatchReplaceClusterNodes/BatchRebootClusterNodes/UpdateCluster-equivalent changes (for ParallelCluster: check for any node replacement/scaling activity, cloud-init-output, clustermgtd, slurm_resume streams). Also get the node capability profile (instance type, GPU count, EFA attached vs max) for each compute node.",
  "resources": ["distributed-training-triage-b200", "AWS ParallelCluster", "us-west-2", "account 111122223333"],
  "timeframe": "2026-09-25T19:35:00Z/2026-09-28T19:35:00Z",
  "criteria": "Per node: (1) which log group + exact log stream carries kernel: lines, (2) hourly coverage proof (lines>0 every hour) across the padded 3-day window, (3) count and codes of any 'NVRM: Xid' occurrences found, (4) any empty/gap hours to flag as Not observable, (5) AWS Health/EC2 status check failures, (6) any node replace/reboot/scaling activity via CloudTrail or clustermgtd logs that could explain a node state change. Do not report 'no errors' for any node without proven coverage -- report Not observable instead where coverage can't be proven."
}", "deep_research": false}, "type": "tool_use"}], "created_at": null}
